WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.845
This briefing comes from Book 00 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

2
00:00:04.845 --> 00:00:08.362
Orientation and Program: the
front door to the series.

3
00:00:08.362 --> 00:00:11.869
It makes the case for modernizing
the client/server estate now,

4
00:00:11.869 --> 00:00:16.251
and sets out the Federal Modernization
Program that the other books carry out.

5
00:00:16.251 --> 00:00:17.800
One point before we start.

6
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:22.037
The workbook is independent technical
guidance, not federal policy.

7
00:00:22.037 --> 00:00:24.802
Where it says a law,
memorandum or directive

8
00:00:24.802 --> 00:00:27.413
requires something, it cites that source.

9
00:00:27.413 --> 00:00:30.908
Everything else, including
the program's phases, gates,

10
00:00:30.908 --> 00:00:35.180
principles and decision rights,
is the workbook's recommendation:

11
00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:39.063
one way to meet those requirements,
not a requirement itself.

12
00:00:39.063 --> 00:00:42.011
I'll take four questions:
why modernize now,

13
00:00:42.011 --> 00:00:45.212
what the program is, who
decides, and how it runs.

14
00:00:45.212 --> 00:00:47.600
Then the decisions to make next.

15
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:49.638
Why modernize now?

16
00:00:49.638 --> 00:00:51.962
Start with what the
directory has been doing.

17
00:00:51.962 --> 00:00:56.260
Most federal estates still run
the client/server model they built

18
00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:00.401
in the early 2000s, with an Active
Directory forest at the center.

19
00:01:00.401 --> 00:01:04.538
Since Windows 2000, the organizational
unit has grouped objects

20
00:01:04.538 --> 00:01:08.600
for delegating administrative
rights and assigning policies.

21
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.638
So placing a user or
computer in the tree decided

22
00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:14.600
several things at once,
with no separate record.

23
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.225
Who administers it, through the permissions
on the organizational unit (OU).

24
00:01:19.225 --> 00:01:24.875
Which policy applies, through Group
Policy linked to sites, domains and OUs.

25
00:01:24.875 --> 00:01:27.200
What it can reach,
through group membership.

26
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.113
How it's named, through DNS
zones stored in the directory.

27
00:01:31.113 --> 00:01:35.237
And whether it's trusted, through
certificates enrolled by Group Policy.

28
00:01:35.237 --> 00:01:36.450
None of that was wrong.

29
00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.175
It just was never written down.

30
00:01:38.175 --> 00:01:40.188
And the cloud doesn't inherit it.

31
00:01:40.188 --> 00:01:43.352
Entra ID's administrative
units can't be nested,

32
00:01:43.352 --> 00:01:47.651
and policy is assigned to groups,
not inherited from a container.

33
00:01:47.651 --> 00:01:51.376
An agency that moves the users
and retires the forest without

34
00:01:51.376 --> 00:01:55.175
recovering those decisions
doesn't modernize its governance.

35
00:01:55.175 --> 00:01:56.590
It deletes it.

36
00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.827
The second reason is the calendar,
and it isn't the agency's any more.

37
00:02:01.827 --> 00:02:04.452
Federal policy sets dates from outside.

38
00:02:04.452 --> 00:02:09.560
M-22-09's Zero Trust actions were
due by the end of fiscal 2024;

39
00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:13.477
the deadline has passed, but
the actions remain in force.

40
00:02:13.477 --> 00:02:20.578
BOD 25-01's mandatory cloud-tenant
policies were due June 20, 2025.

41
00:02:20.578 --> 00:02:24.819
BOD 26-02 requires listed
end-of-support edge

42
00:02:24.819 --> 00:02:29.403
devices to be decommissioned
by February 5, 2027.

43
00:02:29.403 --> 00:02:35.996
M-26-14 rescinded M-21-31, and asks
for an Agency Logging Plan within 90

44
00:02:35.996 --> 00:02:43.414
days of CISA's Logging Reference
Architecture, published on August 20, 2026.

45
00:02:43.414 --> 00:02:47.822
Counted from that date, the
plan is due November 18, 2026;

46
00:02:47.822 --> 00:02:52.327
that's the program's arithmetic,
not a date OMB published.

47
00:02:52.327 --> 00:02:58.203
And FedRAMP's Consolidated Rules
become mandatory on January 1, 2027.

48
00:02:58.203 --> 00:03:00.603
Vendor support adds its own clock.

49
00:03:00.603 --> 00:03:07.214
Windows 10 and Exchange Server 2016
and 2019 ended in October 2025.

50
00:03:07.214 --> 00:03:11.328
Windows Server 2016,
the operating system of

51
00:03:11.328 --> 00:03:15.553
many domain controllers,
ends January 12, 2027.

52
00:03:15.553 --> 00:03:17.390
Nothing fails on a single date.

53
00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:21.004
But every deadline assumes
the agency knows what it runs,

54
00:03:21.004 --> 00:03:23.290
who owns it and what governs it.

55
00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.895
The clocks are often quoted
for more than they say,

56
00:03:26.895 --> 00:03:29.402
so the program plans
against the documents.

57
00:03:29.402 --> 00:03:35.152
FedRAMP certifies cloud services; each
agency still authorizes its own systems.

58
00:03:35.152 --> 00:03:38.590
Moderate is now a
certification class, Class C.

59
00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:42.552
No federal document requires
a Zero Trust maturity stage.

60
00:03:42.552 --> 00:03:48.352
And BOD 25-01 covers cloud tenants,
not the on-premises forest.

61
00:03:48.352 --> 00:03:51.340
Why one program rather
than a set of projects?

62
00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:55.065
Because every project touches
the same implicit decisions.

63
00:03:55.065 --> 00:03:58.603
Intune needs the policy
that GPO links carried.

64
00:03:58.603 --> 00:04:02.990
Retiring directory DNS needs
the names the forest registered.

65
00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:06.861
Rediscovered project by project,
they're answered differently;

66
00:04:06.861 --> 00:04:10.878
owned by no project, they fall
between them until something breaks.

67
00:04:10.878 --> 00:04:14.440
A program can sequence the work
against the external dates.

68
00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:17.015
A collection of projects
can only race them.

69
00:04:17.015 --> 00:04:21.055
The sequence starts with
identity, because policy, access,

70
00:04:21.055 --> 00:04:24.927
devices and data all decide on
the basis of who is asking.

71
00:04:24.927 --> 00:04:28.375
So, three conclusions: fund
the work as one sequence,

72
00:04:28.375 --> 00:04:33.990
hold gates on evidence rather than the
calendar, and be honest about maturity.

73
00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:38.503
A word on how the workbook works,
because this briefing comes from it.

74
00:04:38.503 --> 00:04:41.597
It's organized as eighteen
books, B00 to B17,

75
00:04:41.597 --> 00:04:45.842
numbered along the same
dependency chain: identity first,

76
00:04:45.842 --> 00:04:51.590
then the estate's infrastructure, then
the boundary, assurance and operations.

77
00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:55.834
Every book asks the same four
questions, why, what, who and how,

78
00:04:55.834 --> 00:05:00.165
so a reader who knows one can
find their way through any other.

79
00:05:00.165 --> 00:05:04.418
One book is the authoritative
home for each topic; Book 01,

80
00:05:04.418 --> 00:05:08.853
for example, is canonical for
the program's phases and gates.

81
00:05:08.853 --> 00:05:10.891
Statements come in three kinds.

82
00:05:10.891 --> 00:05:14.839
An external fact is footnoted
to a primary source,

83
00:05:14.839 --> 00:05:19.365
checked on the day the book was
built, here October 21, 2026.

84
00:05:19.365 --> 00:05:22.603
A program design position
is the program's own view,

85
00:05:22.603 --> 00:05:25.378
open to argument rather
than fact-checking.

86
00:05:25.378 --> 00:05:28.358
And an illustrative
example is labeled as one;

87
00:05:28.358 --> 00:05:32.278
the case studies are framing
patterns, not real engagements.

88
00:05:32.278 --> 00:05:34.813
Each chapter keeps a verification file,

89
00:05:34.813 --> 00:05:39.930
with a claims log and a carry-forward
ledger, so every correction is visible.

90
00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:41.817
What is the program?

91
00:05:41.817 --> 00:05:46.095
It starts with a charter: short on
purpose, the page the sponsor signs,

92
00:05:46.095 --> 00:05:49.456
and the page every later
decision is checked against.

93
00:05:49.456 --> 00:05:54.705
Its purpose is to move the organization
from fragmented, legacy-dependent identity,

94
00:05:54.705 --> 00:05:57.480
policy and addressing
systems to a governed,

95
00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:02.730
attribute-driven hybrid-cloud operating
model, without rip-and-replace disruption.

96
00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:07.902
It has six objectives: one authoritative
origin for organizational structure and

97
00:06:07.902 --> 00:06:12.550
identity attributes; governance
rebuilt explicitly, through attributes,

98
00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:17.348
dynamic membership and scoped policy;
drift treated as a classified finding;

99
00:06:17.348 --> 00:06:22.520
change in controlled waves that close with
evidence; domain transformations that

100
00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:27.468
can each proceed on their own schedule;
and continuous, machine-usable evidence.

101
00:06:27.468 --> 00:06:28.979
The scope is the method.

102
00:06:28.979 --> 00:06:31.764
Discovery, the target model, wave plans,

103
00:06:31.764 --> 00:06:35.243
change control, drift
detection and gates are in.

104
00:06:35.243 --> 00:06:37.596
Replacing compliance frameworks,

105
00:06:37.596 --> 00:06:42.630
inline enforcement and the agency's
own cutover execution are out at first.

106
00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:47.096
And one platform fact shapes the
plan: for synced users, the extension

107
00:06:47.096 --> 00:06:52.070
attributes are mastered in Active
Directory and read-only in the cloud.

108
00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:56.033
A sponsor can't sign off an
outcome with no test attached,

109
00:06:56.033 --> 00:06:59.387
so each of the charter's six
success criteria has a measure,

110
00:06:59.387 --> 00:07:02.670
the evidence that shows it, and
the gate where it's checked.

111
00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:07.825
The current-state assessment covers
every forest in scope and all eleven

112
00:07:07.825 --> 00:07:12.557
dependency object types, with zero
open priority-one findings, at G0.

113
00:07:12.557 --> 00:07:16.266
The target model is agreed
when every attribute that

114
00:07:16.266 --> 00:07:20.058
drives access has exactly
one designated source, at G1.

115
00:07:20.058 --> 00:07:22.335
One domain has a complete playbook,

116
00:07:22.335 --> 00:07:26.183
whose entry and exit criteria
are queries the pilot passes.

117
00:07:26.183 --> 00:07:29.386
The change-control pattern
is in use when every pilot

118
00:07:29.386 --> 00:07:32.808
change carries all five clauses
of the change lifecycle.

119
00:07:32.808 --> 00:07:37.408
Every drift class has a detection
signal, a severity and a response.

120
00:07:37.408 --> 00:07:42.003
And leadership can describe the path,
the risks and how success is measured,

121
00:07:42.003 --> 00:07:44.671
without citing internal decision records.

122
00:07:44.671 --> 00:07:46.870
The numbers are illustrative defaults.

123
00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:52.370
The agency sets its own, records them, and
doesn't change them silently afterwards.

124
00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:57.580
And these criteria stop at the pilot;
Chapter 10 measures the rest of the program.

125
00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:03.911
The charter carries six principles in short
form; Chapter 04 sets out all fifteen,

126
00:08:03.911 --> 00:08:07.992
in five groups, each tied to the
book that carries it in depth.

127
00:08:07.992 --> 00:08:12.684
Foundation principles apply to every
change: a single source of truth,

128
00:08:12.684 --> 00:08:16.243
change control with provenance,
and drift detection.

129
00:08:16.243 --> 00:08:17.930
The change rule is strict.

130
00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:22.291
Every move, add, change, deletion
and reset travels five clauses:

131
00:08:22.291 --> 00:08:27.910
it originates with the source of truth,
carries an authoritative authorization,

132
00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:30.678
runs under just-in-time least privilege,

133
00:08:30.678 --> 00:08:34.368
closes with proof, and emits
evidence automatically.

134
00:08:34.368 --> 00:08:37.179
No closure counts unless
it carries its proof.

135
00:08:37.179 --> 00:08:41.618
Directory and structure principles
say what replaces the OU tree.

136
00:08:41.618 --> 00:08:44.936
Devices and policy cover
hybrid join, servers,

137
00:08:44.936 --> 00:08:48.429
Conditional Access and
the Group Policy sunset.

138
00:08:48.429 --> 00:08:52.467
Identity and data cover
the HR feed and SQL Server.

139
00:08:52.467 --> 00:08:54.772
And the program group covers training,

140
00:08:54.772 --> 00:08:57.580
Zero Trust governance
and the overall journey.

141
00:08:57.580 --> 00:09:01.980
Two source rules were corrected
against Microsoft's documentation,

142
00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:06.304
including token lifetimes:
continuous access evaluation revokes

143
00:09:06.304 --> 00:09:10.220
sessions on events, and its
tokens can last up to 28 hours.

144
00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:15.320
The plan follows Book 01's
seven phases and six gates.

145
00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:19.583
Phase 0, Pre-flight, ends
at G0, Ready to Plan.

146
00:09:19.583 --> 00:09:23.032
Foundation ends at G1,
Foundation Complete.

147
00:09:23.032 --> 00:09:26.358
A pilot division ends at G2, Pilot Clean.

148
00:09:26.358 --> 00:09:29.933
General rollout ends at
G3, Rollout Complete.

149
00:09:29.933 --> 00:09:33.507
Legacy retirement ends
at G4, Legacy Quiet.

150
00:09:33.507 --> 00:09:37.695
AD DS retirement ends at G5, AD Retired.

151
00:09:37.695 --> 00:09:41.095
And steady state has no gate;
it's a standing cadence.

152
00:09:41.095 --> 00:09:43.272
Five constraints bind every phase:

153
00:09:43.272 --> 00:09:48.076
changes originate from the authoritative
source and close with provenance,

154
00:09:48.076 --> 00:09:51.529
privilege is time-bound,
copies stay non-authoritative,

155
00:09:51.529 --> 00:09:55.132
drift stays visible, and nothing
requires rip-and-replace.

156
00:09:55.132 --> 00:09:58.895
Underneath them is one rule:
assessment before action.

157
00:09:58.895 --> 00:10:03.305
The program's detailed master plan
numbers its phases differently,

158
00:10:03.305 --> 00:10:07.557
and its illustrative fifty-two
weeks stop short of retiring AD DS.

159
00:10:07.557 --> 00:10:10.870
Large federal estates
typically run multi-year.

160
00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:15.483
Five workstreams run in parallel,
identity, policy, services,

161
00:10:15.483 --> 00:10:21.295
compute and access, and one dependency
never relaxes: PKI waits for identity.

162
00:10:21.295 --> 00:10:25.810
The gates, not the calendar,
decide when the next phase starts.

163
00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:30.110
Inside the program run seven
complementary projects.

164
00:10:30.110 --> 00:10:34.899
Each has its own charter, sponsor,
lead and pilot-scale success criteria,

165
00:10:34.899 --> 00:10:39.282
and each turns a gap the program
finds often into executable work:

166
00:10:39.282 --> 00:10:44.314
identity lifecycle, endpoints, network
and access, continuous monitoring,

167
00:10:44.314 --> 00:10:48.210
privileged access, the data
platform, and the supply chain.

168
00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:53.710
Identity goes first, because everything
else consumes its attributes and owners.

169
00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:57.535
Privileged access is chartered
fifth but starts early,

170
00:10:57.535 --> 00:11:01.360
in the Foundation phase, for
Tier 0 and emergency access.

171
00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:06.434
One detail matters there: Microsoft's
guidance is at least two cloud-only emergency

172
00:11:06.434 --> 00:11:09.816
access accounts with permanent
Global Administrator,

173
00:11:09.816 --> 00:11:14.522
so a target of zero permanent Global
Administrators must exempt them by name.

174
00:11:14.522 --> 00:11:16.261
Two source claims have moved.

175
00:11:16.261 --> 00:11:21.814
OMB rescinded the secure-development
attestation requirement in January 2026,

176
00:11:21.814 --> 00:11:27.285
so a software bill of materials is now an
agency choice backed by contract terms.

177
00:11:27.285 --> 00:11:32.235
And FedRAMP certifies a service, while
the agency authorizes its own system.

178
00:11:32.235 --> 00:11:35.745
Finally, no project creates
a second source of truth,

179
00:11:35.745 --> 00:11:39.220
and no project clears a
program gate on its own.

180
00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:40.995
Who decides?

181
00:11:40.995 --> 00:11:46.070
The workbook uses Book 01's model,
and gives every decision an owner.

182
00:11:46.070 --> 00:11:50.734
The executive sponsor, with the CIO,
owns scope, funding and sequence,

183
00:11:50.734 --> 00:11:54.482
and decides when Active
Directory components retire.

184
00:11:54.482 --> 00:11:57.044
The CISO sets the control requirements.

185
00:11:57.044 --> 00:12:01.582
The steward leads the program office
and owns the plan and the repository.

186
00:12:01.582 --> 00:12:06.784
Gates follow what each one ends:
the steward clears G0, G1 and G4;

187
00:12:06.784 --> 00:12:11.781
the steward and sponsor co-sign
G2; the sponsor and steering

188
00:12:11.781 --> 00:12:16.370
committee clear G3; and the
sponsor and the CIO clear G5.

189
00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:19.957
The program's plans gave three
decisions to the wrong person.

190
00:12:19.957 --> 00:12:23.498
Residual risk belongs to the
authorizing official alone:

191
00:12:23.498 --> 00:12:27.260
under the Risk Management Framework,
no one else can accept it,

192
00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:31.095
and for federal agencies the
role is inherently governmental.

193
00:12:31.095 --> 00:12:33.895
Budget belongs to the sponsor and CIO.

194
00:12:33.895 --> 00:12:35.945
And gates aren't the steward's alone.

195
00:12:35.945 --> 00:12:40.420
The master plan's core team is about
five and a half full-time equivalents.

196
00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:44.344
Budget beyond it, for application
owners, the help desk,

197
00:12:44.344 --> 00:12:48.120
HR, records and the
authorizing official's time.

198
00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:49.808
How does it run?

199
00:12:49.808 --> 00:12:55.129
Pre-flight builds the platform host as
a Tier 0 system and runs the read-only

200
00:12:55.129 --> 00:12:58.991
assessment: the master plan's
twelve domains, plus DHCP,

201
00:12:58.991 --> 00:13:02.595
LDAP bindings and the
service principal name map.

202
00:13:02.595 --> 00:13:07.031
It produces twenty-three deliverables:
fifteen committed before G0,

203
00:13:07.031 --> 00:13:10.533
and eight modernization
plans approved just after it.

204
00:13:10.533 --> 00:13:13.832
G0 passes with no open critical finding.

205
00:13:13.832 --> 00:13:16.712
Foundation enforces the Conditional Access

206
00:13:16.712 --> 00:13:19.819
baseline after at least
seven days in report-only.

207
00:13:19.819 --> 00:13:24.619
The pilot is one division, measured
against eight numeric criteria, such as

208
00:13:24.619 --> 00:13:29.882
authentication success above 99 percent,
and held drift-free for an agreed period.

209
00:13:29.882 --> 00:13:33.545
Rollout then moves in waves
chosen by attribute value.

210
00:13:33.545 --> 00:13:35.483
Retirement takes things away,

211
00:13:35.483 --> 00:13:39.282
so an action is retired only
while its rollback still works.

212
00:13:39.282 --> 00:13:45.319
Domain controller certificates and the
domain's own DNS zones stay until Phase 5.

213
00:13:45.319 --> 00:13:48.880
Consult the records officer
before any hardware goes,

214
00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:52.520
and sanitize retired disks,
which hold password hashes.

215
00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:58.019
In steady state, reassessment runs weekly
and drift detection daily, with a clock

216
00:13:58.019 --> 00:14:03.470
for each severity: by default, a critical
finding is fixed within four hours.

217
00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:06.545
So, four decisions to make next.

218
00:14:06.545 --> 00:14:11.588
Charter the program and name its people,
starting with the sponsor and the steward,

219
00:14:11.588 --> 00:14:16.483
built from the zero trust lead and the
ICAM governance the agency already has.

220
00:14:16.483 --> 00:14:19.526
Scope the read-only
assessment: which forests,

221
00:14:19.526 --> 00:14:22.170
which tenants, which read-only accounts.

222
00:14:22.170 --> 00:14:27.105
Designate the authoritative sources:
normally the HR system for employees,

223
00:14:27.105 --> 00:14:31.570
and a source of their own for contractors,
whom HR usually doesn't hold.

224
00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:36.882
And set the thresholds and evidence rules
before the first change: success criteria,

225
00:14:36.882 --> 00:14:42.270
drift severities and provenance expectations,
recorded and never changed silently.

226
00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:46.634
The recommended first wave is identity
structure and dynamic membership,

227
00:14:46.634 --> 00:14:49.520
because every later wave
scopes itself with them.

228
00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:55.931
And one clock is close: by the program's
count, M-26-14's Agency Logging Plan

229
00:14:55.931 --> 00:15:02.233
is due November 18, 2026, so confirm
that date with the CISO's office now.

230
00:15:02.233 --> 00:15:04.916
Recover the governance
once, in one program,

231
00:15:04.916 --> 00:15:08.794
and let named people make the
decisions the evidence supports.

232
00:15:08.794 --> 00:15:12.995
Book 00 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

233
00:15:12.995 --> 00:15:13.820
Thank you.
